# The Vedic Age: From Pastoral Tribes to Kingdoms

> Between about 1500 and 600 BCE, Indo-Aryan-speaking communities moved from
> the Punjab plains to the Ganga-Yamuna doab — from cattle camps with tribal
> councils to territorial kingdoms with grand sacrifices. Their hymns, the
> Vedas, are the oldest large body of Indian literature.

## 1. The Four Vedas

The Vedas are **shruti** — "heard" revelation — transmitted orally with
extraordinary mnemonic devices (pathas) for centuries before writing.

| Veda | Content | Associated priest | Note |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Rigveda | 1028 hymns in 10 mandalas to gods like Indra and Agni | Hotri (reciter) | Oldest; Gayatri mantra (RV 3.62) |
| Samaveda | Melodies (saman) set to Rigvedic verses | Udgatri (singer) | Root of Indian music |
| Yajurveda | Prose and verse formulas for sacrifice | Adhvaryu (ritualist) | Shukla and Krishna recensions |
| Atharvaveda | Charms, spells, folk medicine | Brahma (overseer) | Closest to popular practice |

## 2. Early Vedic Life (1500-1000 BCE)

The **Rigveda** pictures a pastoral world in the **Sapta Sindhava** — the
"land of seven rivers" (Indus, Jhelum, Chenab, Ravi, Beas, Sutlej, and the
Sarasvati, "the most pious of rivers"). Cattle were the core of wealth.

- **Gavishti**, the common Rigvedic word for war, literally means "search
  for cows."
- Time was measured in **godhuli** (the dust-raising moment of homecoming
  herds); a rich man was a **gomat**.
- Barter, not coins: **nishka** was still a gold ornament, not money.

[example]
A raja's standing was counted in herds, not acres. When hymns pray for
"sons, long life, and cattle," the economic order of the age is on display —
the cow is currency, status, and sacrifice all at once.
[/example]

The era's great conflict was the **Battle of Ten Kings (Dasarajna)** on the
**Parushni (Ravi)**, where **Sudas** of the Bharata tribe defeated a
confederacy including the Purus — the two later merged into the Kuru people.

## 3. Early Political and Social Order

| Institution | Role |
| --- | --- |
| Rajan | Tribal chief, led in war; not absolute |
| Sabha | Council of elders, debated policy |
| Samiti | Wider assembly, chose and advised the rajan |
| Vidatha | Oldest assembly; women could take part |
| Purohita | Chief priest and advisor to the rajan |
| Senani | War commander |
| Gramani | Head of the village (grama) |

Women enjoyed notable standing: seeresses such as **Lopamudra, Apala, and
Ghosha** composed hymns; girls studied the Vedas, chose husbands at
**swayamvara**, and sat in the vidatha. The varna order was present but
flexible — the Rigveda's tenth-mandala **Purusha Sukta** (RV 10.90) gives
the mythic origin of the four varnas from the sacrificed cosmic man.

## 4. Later Vedic Transformation (1000-600 BCE)

Communities moved east into the doab, clearing forest with **krishna ayas**
— iron, appearing from about 1000 BCE at sites such as Atranjikhera — and
leaving the characteristic **Painted Grey Ware (PGW)** pottery.

| Feature | Early Vedic | Later Vedic |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Region | Sapta Sindhava, Punjab | Ganga-Yamuna doab |
| Economy | Pastoral; cattle wealth | Agriculture first; rice and wheat |
| Metal | Copper-bronze (ayas) | Iron (krishna ayas) |
| Pottery | Ochre Coloured Pottery | Painted Grey Ware |
| Chief | Rajan, tribal | King with divine sanction |
| Assemblies | Sabha, samiti, vidatha strong | Declining, priest-dominated |
| Varna | Flexible | Rigid, birth-based |
| Ritual | Simple yajna | Grand royal sacrifices |

## 5. Politics of Sacrifice

Kingship widened into **rashtra** (territorial state) and required ritual
legitimacy from Brahmanas. The great royal rites were the **rajasuya**
(consecration), **ashvamedha** (horse sacrifice claiming supremacy), and
**vajapeya** (chariot-race ritual). New officials appear — **sangrahitri**
(treasurer) and **bhagadugha** (tax collector), signalling regular tribute
(**bhaga**) replacing voluntary **bali**.

[definition]
**Ashvamedha** — the "horse sacrifice." A royal stallion roamed free for a
year under army escort; the lands it crossed had to accept the king's
suzerainty or fight, and the horse's return sealed the claim in ritual.
[/definition]

## 6. The Later Vedic Landscape

The Kuru-Panchala region (around modern Delhi-Doab) was the cultural
heartland; the period's powers are the **Kuru, Panchala, Kosala, Kashi, and
Videha** kingdoms. King **Janaka** of Videha's court hosted the philosophers
**Yajnavalkya** and the women debaters **Gargi and Maitreyi** — a glimpse of
the debate culture that produced the Upanishads.

By now women had lost their place in the assemblies, **gotra** exogamy was
established, and the four ashramas (student, householder, forest-dweller,
renouncer) were systematised in the Jabala Upanishad.

## 7. The Wider Literature

@diagram tree id="vedic-literature" layout="tree" aria="Vedic literature from each Veda to its attached prose texts"
  Vedic Literature -> "Rigveda"
  Vedic Literature -> "Samaveda"
  Vedic Literature -> "Yajurveda"
  Vedic Literature -> "Atharvaveda"
  Rigveda -> "Aitareya Brahmana"
  Rigveda -> "Kaushitaki Brahmana"
  Samaveda -> "Panchavimsha Brahmana"
  Yajurveda -> "Shatapatha Brahmana"
  Atharvaveda -> "Gopatha Brahmana"
  Shatapatha Brahmana -> "Brihadaranyaka Upanishad"
@/diagram

- **Brahmanas**: prose manuals explaining each sacrifice. The vast
  **Shatapatha Brahmana** narrates sage Videha Mathava carrying Agni east —
  the movement of the culture itself.
- **Aranyakas**: "forest treatises" for hermits, bridging ritual and
  philosophy.
- **Upanishads**: 108 in the traditional Muktika list; the philosophical
  core, teaching that **Brahman** (universal soul) and **Atman** (individual
  soul) are one. India's national motto, *Satyameva Jayate* ("truth alone
  triumphs"), comes from the **Mundaka Upanishad**.
- **Vedangas**: six limbs that help study the Veda — Shiksha (phonetics),
  Kalpa (ritual), Vyakarana (grammar), Nirukta (etymology), Chhanda
  (metrics), and Jyotisha (astronomy).

[tip]
Pair each Vedanga with a plain word and the list locks in: Shiksha-sound,
Kalpa-ritual, Vyakarana-grammar, Nirukta-meaning, Chhanda-meter,
Jyotisha-stars. Sound, ritual, grammar, meaning, meter, stars.
[/tip]

## Key Takeaways

- The Vedic Age moved from a pastoral, assembly-governed tribal society in
  the Punjab to agrarian, sacrifice-legitimised kingdoms in the doab.
- Cattle defined early wealth (gavishti); iron and PGW pottery define the
  Later Vedic eastward expansion.
- Women's status, visibly strong in the Rigveda, declined in the Later Vedic
  phase along with the assemblies.
- The Brahmanas, Aranyakas, Upanishads, and six Vedangas grew around the
  four Vedas as ritual and philosophy matured.
